INT. CLOSED PLANETARIUM - OVERNIGHT SLEEPOVER - NIGHT

A great domed theater. Rows of reclined seats, and a projector like a monstrous insect at the center of the floor. The dome above is blank. Six sleeping bags lie in a ragged circle under the projector, and a lantern throws their shadows up the curved wall.

Six teenagers sit on the carpet in pajamas, a paper plate of cold pizza between them, the box lid torn open.

MAEVE ARMSTRONG, 16, the host, cross-legged, in a faded band shirt, plays with a thread on her sleeve. TARIQ BELLAMY, 17, beside her, glances at the exit doors. WINONA SHORTBULL, 16, hugs a pillow, eyes bright. CALLUM TREMBLAY, 17, snaps a glow stick over and over. IMOGEN ABARA, 15, whispers into her hood. And at the edge, a quiet boy, PETROS KALOGEROS, 16, watches the dome with unblinking calm.

The projector hums. A small red light blinks on it, then goes dark.

MAEVE
(to the group)
Okay. Truth or dare.

CALLUM
No.

MAEVE
Yes.

TARIQ
It's two in the morning.

MAEVE
That's when it works.

WINONA
(to Maeve)
Don't be weird.

MAEVE
I'm not weird. I'm hosting.

IMOGEN
(softly, to herself)
It's too cold in here.

PETROS
It's the dome. The air has nowhere to go.

IMOGEN
That's... a thing you know?

PETROS
Mm.

The lantern flickers. Callum snaps the glow stick, and the green light ripples across their faces. The dome is still dark.

MAEVE
Dare. Tariq. Go to the control booth and bring back the key.

TARIQ
What key?

MAEVE
The one in the booth.

TARIQ
There's no key.

MAEVE
Then bring me whatever's there.

Tariq stands. Looks at the dark doors. Then at the others. Nobody encourages him.

TARIQ
(under his breath)
Fine.

He crosses the floor. His footsteps die quickly in the carpet. The others watch him go.

WINONA
(to the group, whispering)
Do you hear that?

CALLUM
What?

WINONA
Humming.

IMOGEN
It's the projector.

WINONA
It's not the projector.

PETROS
It's coming from above.

All six faces lift to the dome. Stars flicker on, one by one, then dozens, then thousands, the whole sky spilling across the curve. No one touches a button. A warm voice murmurs from somewhere, soft as a lullaby, in a language none of them know.

CALLUM
(quietly)
Tariq!

Tariq stands at the booth door, still as a statue, his back to them. He does not answer.

MAEVE
(whispering)
Tariq?

Slowly, he turns. His face is calm. His eyes reflect the stars. He smiles at them.

TARIQ
It's beautiful.

WINONA
Tariq. Come back.

TARIQ
You should see it from here.

IMOGEN
(barely audible)
No.

PETROS
(to the group)
Nobody go to the booth.

The humming rises. Callum drops the glow stick. It rolls into the center of the circle and goes out. In the dark, the stars on the dome begin, ever so slowly, to turn.
